#!/usr/bin/python3
"""waydroid-btd -- drive the host's BlueZ from inside Waydroid's Android.

See docs/50-bluetooth.md for the investigation; the Android half is bt-app/.

WHY A HOST DAEMON, AND NOT ANDROID'S OWN BLUETOOTH

Wi-Fi (docs/28) worked the way it did because the whole Android framework was
already present and dormant in the image and only wanted a vendor HAL. Bluetooth
is not that. Measured on this image:

    pm list packages | grep -i bluetooth    ->  nothing
    ls /apex | grep -i bluetooth            ->  nothing
    service list | grep -i bluetooth        ->  bluetooth_manager

A `bluetooth_manager` binder name published by system_server, and nothing behind
it -- no com.android.bluetooth, no APEX, no Fluoride. There is no framework here
to serve, so a HAL would be the smaller half of a much larger job, and the UI
would still have to be written.

Handing hci0 to the container was rejected for the reason docs/28 rejected it
for Wi-Fi and docs/34 then confirmed by buying a second radio: hci0 is this
machine's only Bluetooth controller and the keyboard the owner types on is
paired to it. Giving it away costs the host its input device to save almost none
of the work.

What is actually needed is narrower than either. Under cage (docs/25) there is
no host UI at all, so there is no way to pair a headset or a keyboard without
ssh-ing in. This daemon exposes BlueZ, and the Android app becomes that UI.

WHY TCP AND NOT BINDER

Every other host daemon here (sensors/, wifi/) speaks libgbinder, because its
client is the Android *framework*, which can only be reached that way. This
daemon's client is an ordinary app, and an ordinary app cannot reach an
arbitrary binder name: ServiceManager.getService() is non-SDK on Android 13, the
name would need an overlay service_contexts entry, untrusted_app would need a
`find` allow rule, and docs/35's dontaudit'ed binder-transfer trap is waiting
underneath all of it. A socket costs none of that.

The container reaches the host at the waydroid0 bridge address (192.168.240.1
here), and firewalld has waydroid0 in the `trusted` zone, so nothing had to be
opened. The wire is newline-delimited JSON: commands up, events down, one
long-lived connection, so a scan list and a pairing passkey arrive live rather
than being polled for. TLS is optional (--tls) and pinned by certificate
fingerprint rather than by CA, since there is no name to verify on a bridge.

DELIBERATELY NOT USED

  * bluetoothctl, scraped. It is a readline UI, its output is not a contract,
    and -- fatally -- it registers its own agent, so a pairing prompt would be
    answered by whichever of us BlueZ made default rather than by the app.
  * busctl --json. Reads fine, but a D-Bus *client* is only half of this: an
    agent has to EXPORT org.bluez.Agent1, and busctl cannot serve an object.
  * a systemd --user unit -- but NOT for the polkit reason docs/46 records for
    udisks2, which was checked and does not apply. BlueZ 5.87 uses no polkit at
    all (there is no org.bluez action, only org.blueman's), and this host's
    /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/bluetooth.conf allows `context="default"` to send
    to org.bluez, so an unprivileged caller really could power the adapter and
    pair. Root is required for exactly one thing: the connection profile below
    goes into the app's private directory, which is owned by the Android app's
    uid and mode 0700. Everything else here would run unprivileged.

python3-dbus and python3-gobject are both already installed on this Atomic host
(bluez pulls them in), so this needs no layered package and no reboot. That is
the only reason it is Python rather than C++ like its two siblings.
"""

